          "final_answer": "The short answer is: **Blood is actually red.**\n\nVeins do not appear blue because the blood inside them is blue. Instead, the blue appearance is an optical illusion caused by how light interacts with the skin and the blood.\n\nHere is the breakdown of why this happens:\n\n**1. Hemoglobin Absorbs Red Light**\nBlood contains a protein called hemoglobin, which carries oxygen. Hemoglobin absorbs red light very well but reflects green and blue light. However, because the skin is thick, the light doesn't just bounce off the surface; it travels deeper into the tissue.\n\n**2. The Depth of Veins vs. Arteries**\n*   **Arteries** are located closer to the surface of the skin. When light hits them, we see the red blood directly reflecting back to our eyes (though it often looks purple or red due to skin tone).\n*   **Veins** are located deeper beneath the skin. The light has to travel through more layers of skin and tissue to reach the veins.\n\n**3. Light Scattering and Absorption**\nWhen white light hits your skin, it is scattered. However, as that light travels deeper into the skin to reach the veins:\n*   **Red light** is absorbed by the hemoglobin in the blood within the vein.\n*   **Blue light** passes through the skin layers more easily and reflects back toward your eyes.\n\nBecause the blue light is the one that bounces back to your eye, your brain interprets the color as blue.\n\n**4. Skin Color Matters**\nThis effect is stronger in people with lighter skin tones. In darker skin tones, melanin absorbs more of the light, which can sometimes make veins appear purple or dark red rather than bright blue.\n\n**Summary**\nVeins look blue because the skin scatters and filters the light coming from the deep veins. The red blood is absorbed by the tissue, while the blue light reflects back to your eyes, creating the illusion of a blue vessel.",
