          "prompt": "Find $x$ such that $\\lceil x \\rceil + x = \\dfrac{23}{7}$. Express $x$ as a common fraction.",
          "final_answer": "Let $n = \\lceil x \\rceil$. By definition of the ceiling function, $n$ is an integer such that $n - 1 < x \\le n$.\nThe given equation is:\n$$n + x = \\frac{23}{7}$$\nSolving for $x$, we get:\n$$x = \\frac{23}{7} - n$$\nSubstitute this expression for $x$ into the inequality $n - 1 < x \\le n$:\n$$n - 1 < \\frac{23}{7} - n \\le n$$\n\nThis compound inequality gives us two separate inequalities to solve for the integer $n$:\n\n1.  $\\frac{23}{7} - n \\le n$\n    $$\\frac{23}{7} \\le 2n$$\n    $$n \\ge \\frac{23}{14}$$\n    $$n \\ge 1.64...$$\n    Since $n$ is an integer, $n \\ge 2$.\n\n2.  $n - 1 < \\frac{23}{7} - n$\n    $$2n < 1 + \\frac{23}{7}$$\n    $$2n < \\frac{7}{7} + \\frac{23}{7}$$\n    $$2n < \\frac{30}{7}$$\n    $$n < \\frac{15}{7}$$\n    $$n < 2.14...$$\n    Since $n$ is an integer, $n \\le 2$.\n\nCombining the results from both inequalities ($n \\ge 2$ and $n \\le 2$), we find that the only possible integer value for $n$ is:\n$$n = 2$$\n\nNow, substitute $n = 2$ back into the equation for $x$:\n$$x = \\frac{23}{7} - 2$$\n$$x = \\frac{23}{7} - \\frac{14}{7}$$\n$$x = \\frac{9}{7}$$\n\nWe can verify this solution:\n$\\lceil \\frac{9}{7} \\rceil = \\lceil 1.285... \\rceil = 2$.\n$2 + \\frac{9}{7} = \\frac{14}{7} + \\frac{9}{7} = \\frac{23}{7}$.\nThe solution is correct.\n\n$x = \\frac{9}{7}$",
