          "case_id": "math-exponential-offer-021",
          "dimension": "math",
          "prompt": "Mike paid $\\$1.25$ for a stamp three years ago. He was just offered double that amount for the stamp. Assuming the stamp's offer price doubles every three years, how many dollars will he be offered in 12 more years?",
          "final_answer": "Here is the step-by-step calculation to determine the answer:\n\n1.  **Determine the current offer price:**\n    Mike paid $\\$1.25$ three years ago. He was just offered double that amount.\n    $$ \\$1.25 \\times 2 = \\$2.50 $$\n    So, the current offer price is $\\$2.50$.\n\n2.  **Determine the number of doubling periods:**\n    The problem states the offer price doubles every three years. We need to find the price in 12 more years.\n    $$ 12 \\text{ years} / 3 \\text{ years per period} = 4 \\text{ periods} $$\n\n3.  **Calculate the future offer price:**\n    Since the price doubles 4 times, we multiply the current price by $2$ four times (or by $2^4$, which is $16$).\n    $$ \\$2.50 \\times 2^4 = \\$2.50 \\times 16 $$\n    $$ \\$2.50 \\times 16 = \\$40.00 $$\n\nHe will be offered **$\\$40$**.",
