          "final_answer": "We are asked to find $x$ such that\n$$\\lceil x \\rceil + x = \\frac{23}{7}$$\n\nLet $n = \\lceil x \\rceil$. Since $n$ is the ceiling of $x$, $n$ must be an integer.\nSubstituting $n$ into the equation, we get:\n$$n + x = \\frac{23}{7}$$\nWe can express $x$ in terms of $n$:\n$$x = \\frac{23}{7} - n$$\n\nBy the definition of the ceiling function, $n = \\lceil x \\rceil$ implies the inequality:\n$$n - 1 < x \\le n$$\n\nSubstitute the expression for $x$ into this inequality:\n$$n - 1 < \\frac{23}{7} - n \\le n$$\n\nWe solve this compound inequality for the integer $n$.\n\n1. Solve the left inequality: $n - 1 < \\frac{23}{7} - n$\n$$2n - 1 < \\frac{23}{7}$$\n$$2n < \\frac{23}{7} + 1$$\n$$2n < \\frac{23 + 7}{7}$$\n$$2n < \\frac{30}{7}$$\n$$n < \\frac{15}{7}$$\nSince $\\frac{15}{7} = 2 + \\frac{1}{7} \\approx 2.14$, $n$ must be an integer less than $2.14$.\n\n2. Solve the right inequality: $\\frac{23}{7} - n \\le n$\n$$\\frac{23}{7} \\le 2n$$\n$$n \\ge \\frac{23}{14}$$\nSince $\\frac{23}{14} = 1 + \\frac{9}{14} \\approx 1.64$, $n$ must be an integer greater than or equal to $1.64$.\n\nWe need an integer $n$ that satisfies both $n < 2.14$ and $n \\ge 1.64$. The only integer that satisfies both conditions is $n=2$.\n\nNow we substitute $n=2$ back into the expression for $x$:\n$$x = \\frac{23}{7} - n$$\n$$x = \\frac{23}{7} - 2$$\n$$x = \\frac{23}{7} - \\frac{14}{7}$$\n$$x = \\frac{9}{7}$$\n\nWe verify the solution:\nIf $x = 9/7$, then $\\lceil x \\rceil = \\lceil 9/7 \\rceil = 2$.\n$\\lceil x \\rceil + x = 2 + \\frac{9}{7} = \\frac{14}{7} + \\frac{9}{7} = \\frac{23}{7}$.\n\nThe solution is correct.\n\n$x$ expressed as a common fraction is $\\frac{9}{7}$.",
