          "case_id": "math-exponential-offer-021",
          "dimension": "math",
          "prompt": "Mike paid $\\$1.25$ for a stamp three years ago. He was just offered double that amount for the stamp. Assuming the stamp's offer price doubles every three years, how many dollars will he be offered in 12 more years?",
          "final_answer": "This is a problem of exponential growth.\n\nHere is the step-by-step calculation:\n\n**1. Determine the current price (The starting point):**\n* Initial price (3 years ago): $\\$1.25$\n* Current price (after 3 years, which is double the initial price): $\\$1.25 \\times 2 = \\$2.50$\n\n**2. Determine the number of doubling periods:**\n* The price doubles every 3 years.\n* The time frame is 12 more years.\n* Number of periods = $\\frac{12 \\text{ years}}{3 \\text{ years/period}} = 4$ periods\n\n**3. Calculate the final price:**\nWe start with the current price ($\\$2.50$) and double it 4 times:\n\n* **Start:** $\\$2.50$\n* **After 3 years (Period 1):** $\\$2.50 \\times 2 = \\$5.00$\n* **After 6 years (Period 2):** $\\$5.00 \\times 2 = \\$10.00$\n* **After 9 years (Period 3):** $\\$10.00 \\times 2 = \\$20.00$\n* **After 12 years (Period 4):** $\\$20.00 \\times 2 = \\$40.00$\n\nAlternatively, using the formula:\n$$\\text{Final Price} = \\text{Current Price} \\times 2^{\\text{Number of Periods}}$$\n$$\\text{Final Price} = \\$2.50 \\times 2^4$$\n$$\\text{Final Price} = \\$2.50 \\times 16$$\n$$\\text{Final Price} = \\$40.00$$\n\nMike will be offered **\\$40.00** in 12 more years.",
