          "case_id": "math-piecewise-continuity-000",
          "dimension": "math",
          "prompt": "Let \\[f(x) = \\left\\{\n\\begin{array}{cl} ax+3, &\\text{ if }x>2, \\\\\nx-5 &\\text{ if } -2 \\le x \\le 2, \\\\\n2x-b &\\text{ if } x <-2.\n\\end{array}\n\\right.\\]Find $a+b$ if the piecewise function is continuous (which means that its graph can be drawn without lifting your pencil from the paper).",
          "final_answer": "For the piecewise function $f(x)$ to be continuous everywhere, it must be continuous at the transition points, which are $x=2$ and $x=-2$.\n\nWe need to ensure that the limits from the left and the right match the function value at these points.\n\n### 1. Continuity at $x=2$\n\nFor continuity at $x=2$, we must have:\n$$\\lim_{x \\to 2^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 2^+} f(x) = f(2)$$\n\n1. **Value at $x=2$ (using the middle definition):**\n   $$f(2) = 2 - 5 = -3$$\n\n2. **Limit from the left ($x \\to 2^-$):**\n   $$\\lim_{x \\to 2^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 2^-} (x-5) = 2 - 5 = -3$$\n\n3. **Limit from the right ($x \\to 2^+$):**\n   $$\\lim_{x \\to 2^+} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 2^+} (ax+3) = a(2) + 3 = 2a + 3$$\n\nSetting the limits equal:\n$$2a + 3 = -3$$\n$$2a = -6$$\n$$a = -3$$\n\n### 2. Continuity at $x=-2$\n\nFor continuity at $x=-2$, we must have:\n$$\\lim_{x \\to -2^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to -2^+} f(x) = f(-2)$$\n\n1. **Value at $x=-2$ (using the middle definition):**\n   $$f(-2) = -2 - 5 = -7$$\n\n2. **Limit from the left ($x \\to -2^-$):**\n   $$\\lim_{x \\to -2^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to -2^-} (2x-b) = 2(-2) - b = -4 - b$$\n\n3. **Limit from the right ($x \\to -2^+$):**\n   $$\\lim_{x \\to -2^+} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to -2^+} (x-5) = -2 - 5 = -7$$\n\nSetting the limits equal:\n$$-4 - b = -7$$\n$$-b = -7 + 4$$\n$$-b = -3$$\n$$b = 3$$\n\n### 3. Finding $a+b$\n\nWe found $a = -3$ and $b = 3$.\n$$a+b = -3 + 3 = 0$$",
