          "case_id": "math-quadratic-interval-006",
          "dimension": "math",
          "prompt": "For what values of $x$ is it true that $x^2 - 5x - 4 \\le 10$? Express your answer in interval notation.",
          "final_answer": "We rewrite the inequality  \n\n\\[\nx^2-5x-4\\le 10\n\\]\n\nby moving all terms to one side:\n\n\\[\nx^2-5x-4-10\\le 0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x^2-5x-14\\le 0.\n\\]\n\nSolve the quadratic equation \\(x^2-5x-14=0\\):\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = (-5)^2-4(1)(-14)=25+56=81,\\qquad \\sqrt\\Delta =9,\n\\]\n\\[\nx=\\frac{5\\pm 9}{2}\\;\\Longrightarrow\\; x=7\\text{ or }x=-2.\n\\]\n\nSince the coefficient of \\(x^2\\) is positive, the parabola opens upward, so the expression \\(x^2-5x-14\\) is non‑positive exactly between its roots (including the roots).  \n\nThus the solution set is the closed interval from \\(-2\\) to \\(7\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{[-2,7]}\n\\]",
