          "final_answer": "The function is continuous at the boundary points \\(x=2\\) and \\(x=-2\\).\n\n- At \\(x=2\\):  \n  \\(\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to2^+}f(x)=a(2)+3=2a+3\\) must equal \\(f(2)=2-5=-3\\).  \n  Hence \\(2a+3=-3\\Rightarrow a=-3\\).\n\n- At \\(x=-2\\):  \n  \\(\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to-2^-}f(x)=2(-2)-b=-4-b\\) must equal \\(f(-2)=-2-5=-7\\).  \n  Hence \\(-4-b=-7\\Rightarrow b=3\\).\n\nThus \\(a+b=-3+3=0\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]",
