          "final_answer": "To find \\(a + b\\) for the given piecewise function \\(f(x)\\), we need to determine the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) such that the function is continuous. A function is continuous at a point if the left-hand limit, the right-hand limit, and the function value at that point are equal.\n\nThe function is defined as:\n\\[ f(x) = \\begin{cases} \nax + 3, & \\text{if } x > 2, \\\\\nx - 5, & \\text{if } -2 \\le x \\le 2, \\\\\n2x - b, & \\text{if } x < 2.\n\\end{cases} \\]\n\nFor \\(f(x)\\) to be continuous at \\(x = 2\\), the following conditions must hold:\n1. \\(f(2)\\) must be defined.\n2. The left-hand limit \\(f'(2)\\) must equal the right-hand limit \\(f'(2)\\).\n3. The function value \\(f(2)\\) must equal \\(f'(2)\\).\n\nLet's evaluate \\(f(2)\\) for each case:\n\n1. **Case 1: \\(x > 2\\)**\n   \\[ f(2) = a \\cdot 2 + 3 = 2a + 3 \\]\n\n2. **Case 2: \\(-2 \\le x \\le 2\\)**\n   \\[ f(x) = x - 5 \\]\n   \\[ f'(x) = 1 \\]\n   \\[ f'(2) = 1 \\]\n\n3. **Case 3: \\(x < 2\\)**\n   \\[ f(x) = 2x - b \\]\n   \\[ f'(x) = 2 \\]\n   \\[ f'(2) = 2 \\]\n\nFor continuity at \\(x = 2\\), we require:\n\\[ f(2) = f'(2) \\]\n\\[ 2a + 3 = 2 \\]\n\\[ 2a = -1 \\]\n\\[ a = -\\frac{1}{2} \\]\n\nNow, we need to find \\(b\\) such that \\(f(2) = f'(2)\\):\n\\[ 2a + 3 = 2 \\]\n\\[ 2 \\left( -\\frac{1}{2} \\right) + 3 = 2 \\]\n\\[ -1 + 3 = 2 \\]\n\\[ 2 = 2 \\]\n\nThis condition is satisfied for any value of \\(b\\). Therefore, \\(a = -\\frac{1}{2}\\) and \\(b\\) can be any real number.\n\nThus, \\(a + b = -\\frac{1}{2} + b\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{-\\frac{1}{2} + b} \\]",
