          "final_answer": "To determine the greatest integer value of $b$ for which the expression $\\frac{9x^3+4x^2+11x+7}{x^2+bx+8}$ has a domain of all real numbers, we need to ensure that the denominator $x^2 + bx + 8$ does not have any real roots.\n\nThe discriminant of the quadratic equation $x^2 + bx + 8$ is given by $b^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 8 = b^2 - 32$. For the quadratic to have real roots, the discriminant must be non-negative:\n\n$$b^2 - 32 \\geq 0$$\n\nSolving for $b$, we get:\n\n$$b^2 \\geq 32$$\n$$b \\geq \\sqrt{32} = 4\\sqrt{2}$$\n\nSince $b$ must be an integer, the greatest integer value of $b$ for which the expression has a domain of all real numbers is $\\boxed{4}$.",
