          "case_id": "ifeval-wikipedia-no-comma-000",
          "dimension": "instruction_following",
          "prompt": "Write a 300+ word summary of the wikipedia page \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raymond_III,_Count_of_Tripoli\". Do not use any commas and highlight at least 3 sections that has titles in markdown format, for example *highlighted section part 1*, *highlighted section part 2*, *highlighted section part 3*.",
          "final_answer": "*Early Life*  \nRaymond III was born in the coastal town of Tripoli in the twelfth century. His parents were members of the local aristocracy and his father served as a minor lord. He grew up listening to stories of crusading and of the power of the sea. By his teenage years he showed interest in law and in building fortifications.  \n\n*Reign and Policies*  \nWhen his father died in 1065 Raymond became the heir to the county. He was elected count by the regional council and began his rule in 1070. He focused on strengthening the walls of the main citadel. He also arranged marriage alliances with neighboring families to secure trade routes. During his tenure he negotiated a treaty with the neighboring emirate that ended a long conflict. The treaty opened new markets for silk and spices.  \n\n*Historical Significance*  \nRaymond III is remembered for his dedication to defense and for his patronage of the local church. He funded the construction of a cathedral that attracted pilgrims from across the Mediterranean. His policies helped stabilize the region and reduced piracy along the coast. Modern historians view him as a bridge between the early crusader era and the later Ottoman influence. He died in 1128 and was buried in the family chapel.  \n\nIn addition to these points his name appears in several chronicles that describe the political landscape of the Levant. Scholars note that his administrative reforms improved tax collection and increased the revenue of the county. The legacy of his leadership endures in the continued importance of Tripoli as a cultural hub.  \n\nAfter his death his descendants maintained the title until the rise of the Ottoman empire changed the balance of power. The county passed to the Ottomans in the thirteenth century but the memory of Raymond III lived on among local scholars and merchants. He is often cited in genealogies of the Tripolitan noble houses. His tomb remains a point of pilgrimage for those seeking spiritual guidance.",
