          "case_id": "math-ceiling-fraction-002",
          "dimension": "math",
          "prompt": "Find $x$ such that $\\lceil x \\rceil + x = \\dfrac{23}{7}$. Express $x$ as a common fraction.",
          "final_answer": "The ceiling function \\(\\lceil x \\rceil\\) is an integer \\(n\\). By definition, \\(n-1 < x \\le n\\). The given equation is\n\n\\[\n\\lceil x \\rceil + x = \\frac{23}{7} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad n + x = \\frac{23}{7}.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{23}{7} - n.\n\\]\n\nSubstitute into the inequality bounds:\n\n\\[\nn-1 < \\frac{23}{7} - n \\le n.\n\\]\n\nConsider the right part first:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{23}{7} - n \\le n \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; \\frac{23}{7} \\le 2n \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; n \\ge \\frac{23}{14} \\approx 1.6429.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n\\) is an integer, \\(n \\ge 2\\).\n\nNow the left part:\n\n\\[\nn-1 < \\frac{23}{7} - n \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; 2n - 1 < \\frac{23}{7} \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; 2n < \\frac{23}{7} + 1 = \\frac{30}{7} \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; n < \\frac{15}{7} \\approx 2.1429.\n\\]\n\nFor integer \\(n\\), \\(n < 2.1429\\) gives \\(n \\le 2\\).\n\nCombining \\(n \\ge 2\\) and \\(n \\le 2\\) yields \\(n = 2\\).\n\nThen\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{23}{7} - 2 = \\frac{23}{7} - \\frac{14}{7} = \\frac{9}{7}.\n\\]\n\nCheck: \\(\\lceil \\frac{9}{7} \\rceil = 2\\) and \\(2 + \\frac{9}{7} = \\frac{14}{7} + \\frac{9}{7} = \\frac{23}{7}\\). The condition \\(1 < \\frac{9}{7} \\le 2\\) holds. Thus the solution is valid.\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{9}{7}}\n\\]",
