          "prompt": "Suppose the roots of the polynomial $x^2 - mx + n$ are positive prime integers (not necessarily distinct). Given that $m < 20,$ how many possible values of $n$ are there?",
          "final_answer": "The roots are positive prime integers \\(p\\) and \\(q\\). By Vieta's formulas,  \n\\[\np+q = m,\\qquad pq = n,\n\\]  \nwith the condition \\(m < 20\\), i.e., \\(p+q \\le 19\\) (since \\(m\\) is an integer).  \n\nWe need all unordered pairs of primes \\((p,q)\\) (allowing \\(p=q\\)) such that \\(p+q \\le 19\\). Enumerating these pairs:\n\n- \\(p=2\\): \\(q = 2,3,5,7,11,13,17\\) → 7 pairs  \n- \\(p=3\\): \\(q = 3,5,7,11,13\\) → 5 pairs  \n- \\(p=5\\): \\(q = 5,7,11,13\\) → 4 pairs  \n- \\(p=7\\): \\(q = 7,11\\) → 2 pairs  \n\nTotal pairs = \\(7+5+4+2 = 18\\).\n\nEach pair gives a product \\(n = pq\\). Because prime factorization is unique, different pairs yield different products. Hence there are 18 distinct values of \\(n\\).\n\nThe corresponding \\(n\\) values are:  \n\\(4,6,10,14,22,26,34,9,15,21,33,39,25,35,55,65,49,77\\).\n\nThus the number of possible values of \\(n\\) is \\(\\boxed{18}\\).",
