          "dimension": "math",
          "prompt": "Find the greatest integer value of $b$ for which the expression $\\frac{9x^3+4x^2+11x+7}{x^2+bx+8}$ has a domain of all real numbers.",
          "final_answer": "The domain of the rational function \\(\\frac{9x^3+4x^2+11x+7}{x^2+bx+8}\\) consists of all real numbers except those that make the denominator zero. For the domain to be all real numbers, the denominator \\(x^2+bx+8\\) must never equal zero for any real \\(x\\). This requires the quadratic to have no real roots, i.e., its discriminant must be negative:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = b^2 - 4\\cdot1\\cdot8 = b^2 - 32 < 0.\n\\]\n\nSolving \\(b^2 < 32\\) gives \\(|b| < \\sqrt{32} = 4\\sqrt{2} \\approx 5.65685\\). Thus \\(b\\) must satisfy \\(-4\\sqrt{2} < b < 4\\sqrt{2}\\). The greatest integer in this interval is \\(5\\), since \\(5 < 4\\sqrt{2}\\) and \\(6 > 4\\sqrt{2}\\).\n\nCheck: For \\(b=5\\), \\(\\Delta = 25 - 32 = -7 < 0\\), so no real zeros. For \\(b=6\\), \\(\\Delta = 36 - 32 = 4 > 0\\), giving real zeros, so the domain would exclude some real numbers. Therefore, the greatest integer \\(b\\) is \\(5\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{5}\n\\]",
