          "case_id": "math-ceiling-fraction-002",
          "dimension": "math",
          "prompt": "Find $x$ such that $\\lceil x \\rceil + x = \\dfrac{23}{7}$. Express $x$ as a common fraction.",
          "final_answer": "To solve the equation \\(\\lceil x \\rceil + x = \\dfrac{23}{7}\\), we start by letting \\(n = \\lceil x \\rceil\\). This means \\(n\\) is an integer and \\(n - 1 < x \\leq n\\). Substituting \\(n\\) into the equation, we get:\n\n\\[\nn + x = \\frac{23}{7}\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(x\\), we have:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{23}{7} - n\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n - 1 < x \\leq n\\), substituting \\(x = \\frac{23}{7} - n\\) into the inequality gives:\n\n\\[\nn - 1 < \\frac{23}{7} - n \\leq n\n\\]\n\nWe split this into two inequalities:\n\n1. \\(n - 1 < \\frac{23}{7} - n\\)\n2. \\(\\frac{23}{7} - n \\leq n\\)\n\nFor the first inequality:\n\n\\[\nn - 1 < \\frac{23}{7} - n\n\\]\n\nAdding \\(n\\) to both sides:\n\n\\[\n2n - 1 < \\frac{23}{7}\n\\]\n\nAdding 1 to both sides:\n\n\\[\n2n < \\frac{30}{7}\n\\]\n\nDividing by 2:\n\n\\[\nn < \\frac{15}{7} \\approx 2.142\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n\\) is an integer, the possible values for \\(n\\) are 2.\n\nFor the second inequality:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{23}{7} - n \\leq n\n\\]\n\nAdding \\(n\\) to both sides:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{23}{7} \\leq 2n\n\\]\n\nDividing by 2:\n\n\\[\nn \\geq \\frac{23}{14} \\approx 1.642\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n\\) is an integer, the possible values for \\(n\\) are 2.\n\nThus, \\(n = 2\\). Substituting \\(n = 2\\) back into the equation for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{23}{7} - 2 = \\frac{23}{7} - \\frac{14}{7} = \\frac{9}{7}\n\\]\n\nWe verify that \\(x = \\frac{9}{7}\\) satisfies the original equation:\n\n\\[\n\\lceil \\frac{9}{7} \\rceil + \\frac{9}{7} = 2 + \\frac{9}{7} = \\frac{14}{7} + \\frac{9}{7} = \\frac{23}{7}\n\\]\n\nThus, the solution is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{9}{7}}\n\\]",
