          "case_id": "math-prime-roots-020",
          "dimension": "math",
          "prompt": "Suppose the roots of the polynomial $x^2 - mx + n$ are positive prime integers (not necessarily distinct). Given that $m < 20,$ how many possible values of $n$ are there?",
          "final_answer": "The polynomial \\(x^2 - mx + n\\) has roots that are positive prime integers. By Vieta's formulas, if the roots are \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) (primes), then \\(m = p + q\\) and \\(n = pq\\). The condition \\(m < 20\\) implies \\(p + q < 20\\). We need to count the distinct values of \\(n = pq\\) for all pairs of positive primes \\(p, q\\) (allowing \\(p = q\\)) such that \\(p + q < 20\\).\n\nList all pairs of primes (with \\(p \\le q\\) to avoid double counting) and compute \\(p + q\\) and \\(pq\\):\n\n- \\(p = 2\\): \\(q = 3,5,7,11,13,17\\) → sums: 5,7,9,13,15,19 → \\(n = 6,15,21,33,35,51\\)\n- \\(p = 3\\): \\(q = 5,7,11,13,17\\) → sums: 8,10,14,16,20 → \\(n = 15,21,33,39,51\\)\n- \\(p = 5\\): \\(q = 7,11,13,17\\) → sum 12,16,18,22 → \\(n = 35,55,65,85\\)\n- \\(p = 7\\): \\(q = 11,13,17\\) → sum 18,20,24 → \\(n = 77,91,119\\)\n- \\(p = 11\\): \\(q = 13,17\\) → sum 24,28 → \\(n = 143,187\\) (both \\(\\ge 20\\), excluded)\n\nThe distinct values of \\(n\\) are: 6, 15, 21, 33, 35, 51, 77, 91, 119, 143, 187. That is 11 values.\n\nThus, there are 11 possible values of \\(n\\).\n\n\\boxed{11}",
