          "prompt": "Find the greatest integer value of $b$ for which the expression $\\frac{9x^3+4x^2+11x+7}{x^2+bx+8}$ has a domain of all real numbers.",
          "final_answer": "The expression \\(\\frac{9x^3+4x^2+11x+7}{x^2+bx+8}\\) is defined for all real numbers if and only if the denominator \\(x^2+bx+8\\) has no real roots. The discriminant of the quadratic is \\(\\Delta = b^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 8 = b^2 - 32\\). For no real roots, we need \\(\\Delta < 0\\), i.e., \\(b^2 < 32\\). The greatest integer \\(b\\) satisfying this is \\(b = 5\\) because \\(5^2 = 25 < 32\\) and \\(6^2 = 36 > 32\\). Therefore, the greatest integer value of \\(b\\) is \\(5\\).\n\n\\boxed{5}",
