          "final_answer": "Based on current scientific consensus, **MSG (monosodium glutamate) has not been proven to be harmful to humans in small doses.** Here’s a breakdown:\n\n### Key Points:\n1. **Safety at Normal Dietary Levels**:  \n   - Major health organizations (e.g., FDA, EFSA, WHO) consider MSG safe for consumption as part of a balanced diet.  \n   - Studies show no adverse effects from consuming typical amounts found in foods like soy sauce, sushi seasoning, or processed meats.\n\n2. **Mechanism & Processing**:  \n   - MSG is rapidly absorbed and metabolized by the body. Even high intakes are quickly cleared.  \n   - Some research suggests it may have mild effects on blood pressure or glucose regulation, but these are not linked to toxicity at normal levels.\n\n3. **“Small Doses” Clarification**:  \n   - While extremely high doses (e.g., >100 mg/day) *might* theoretically affect certain individuals (e.g., those with kidney issues), this is not considered a concern for healthy adults.  \n   - The term “small doses” usually refers to typical food amounts (e.g., 10–50 mg per serving), which are well within safety limits.\n\n4. **Regulatory Approval**:  \n   - Many countries require MSG approval for food products, reflecting its acceptance as a safe additive.\n\n### Conclusion:\nFor most people, MSG is safe when consumed as part of a varied diet. However, if you have specific health conditions (e.g., kidney disease), consulting a healthcare provider is advisable. Always check labels for added MSG content, especially in processed foods.",
