          "dimension": "math",
          "prompt": "Let \\[f(x) = \\left\\{\n\\begin{array}{cl} ax+3, &\\text{ if }x>2, \\\\\nx-5 &\\text{ if } -2 \\le x \\le 2, \\\\\n2x-b &\\text{ if } x <-2.\n\\end{array}\n\\right.\\]Find $a+b$ if the piecewise function is continuous (which means that its graph can be drawn without lifting your pencil from the paper).",
          "final_answer": "The function is continuous at \\(x = 2\\) and \\(x = -2\\).\n\nAt \\(x = 2\\):\n\\[\n\\lim_{x \\to 2^-} f(x) = 2 - 5 = -3, \\quad \\lim_{x \\to 2^+} f(x) = a(2) + 3 = 2a + 3.\n\\]\nSet them equal: \\(2a + 3 = -3 \\Rightarrow 2a = -6 \\Rightarrow a = -3\\).\n\nAt \\(x = -2\\):\n\\[\n\\lim_{x \\to -2^-} f(x) = 2(-2) - b = -4 - b, \\quad \\lim_{x \\to -2^+} f(x) = (-2) - 5 = -7.\n\\]\nSet them equal: \\(-4 - b = -7 \\Rightarrow -b = -3 \\Rightarrow b = 3\\).\n\nThen \\(a + b = -3 + 3 = 0\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]",
