          "dimension": "math",
          "prompt": "Let \\[f(x) =\n\\begin{cases}\n2x^2 - 3&\\text{if } x\\le 2, \\\\\nax + 4 &\\text{if } x>2.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]Find $a$ if the graph of $y=f(x)$ is continuous (which means the graph can be drawn without lifting your pencil from the paper).",
          "final_answer": "The function \\(f(x)\\) is defined piecewise. For continuity at \\(x = 2\\), the left-hand limit must equal the right-hand limit and equal \\(f(2)\\).\n\nCompute \\(f(2)\\) using the first piece:\n\\[\nf(2) = 2(2)^2 - 3 = 2 \\cdot 4 - 3 = 8 - 3 = 5.\n\\]\n\nThe left-hand limit as \\(x \\to 2^-\\) is also \\(5\\) because the expression \\(2x^2 - 3\\) is continuous at \\(x = 2\\).\n\nThe right-hand limit as \\(x \\to 2^+\\) uses the second piece:\n\\[\n\\lim_{x \\to 2^+} (ax + 4) = 2a + 4.\n\\]\n\nSet this equal to \\(f(2)\\):\n\\[\n2a + 4 = 5 \\implies 2a = 1 \\implies a = \\frac{1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(a\\) that makes the graph continuous is \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\).\n\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}}",
