          "prompt": "For all real numbers $r$ and $s$, define the mathematical operation $\\#$ such that the following conditions apply: $r\\ \\#\\ 0 = r, r\\ \\#\\ s = s\\ \\#\\ r$, and $(r + 1)\\ \\#\\ s = (r\\ \\#\\ s) + s + 1$. What is the value of $11\\ \\#\\ 5$?",
          "final_answer": "The operation \\(\\#\\) is defined by the conditions:\n\n- \\(r \\# 0 = r\\)\n- \\(r \\# s = s \\# r\\)\n- \\((r+1) \\# s = (r \\# s) + s + 1\\)\n\nFrom the first condition, \\(r \\# 0 = r\\). Setting \\(r = 0\\) gives \\(0 \\# 0 = 0\\), and using commutativity, \\(0 \\# s = s\\).\n\nUsing the third condition with \\(r = 0\\):\n\\[\n(0+1) \\# s = (0 \\# s) + s + 1 \\implies 1 \\# s = s + s + 1 = 2s + 1.\n\\]\n\nThen for \\(r = 1\\):\n\\[\n1 \\# s = 2s + 1.\n\\]\n\nBy induction, assuming \\(f(r, s) = (r+1)s + r\\) holds for some \\(r\\), we have\n\\[\nf(r+1, s) = f(r, s) + s + 1 = (r+1)s + r + s + 1 = (r+2)s + (r+1).\n\\]\nThis matches the form \\((r+2)s + (r+1)\\), so the formula is valid for all \\(r\\).\n\nThus the operation is defined as\n\\[\nr \\# s = (r+1)s + r.\n\\]\n\nNow compute \\(11 \\# 5\\):\n\\[\n11 \\# 5 = (11+1) \\cdot 5 + 11 = 12 \\cdot 5 + 11 = 60 + 11 = 71.\n\\]\n\n\\boxed{71}",
