          "case_id": "humaneval-make-palindrome-010",
          "dimension": "coding",
          "prompt": "\n\ndef is_palindrome(string: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\" Test if given string is a palindrome \"\"\"\n    return string == string[::-1]\n\n\ndef make_palindrome(string: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\" Find the shortest palindrome that begins with a supplied string.\n    Algorithm idea is simple:\n    - Find the longest postfix of supplied string that is a palindrome.\n    - Append to the end of the string reverse of a string prefix that comes before the palindromic suffix.\n    >>> make_palindrome('')\n    ''\n    >>> make_palindrome('cat')\n    'catac'\n    >>> make_palindrome('cata')\n    'catac'\n    \"\"\"\n",
          "final_answer": "**Solution Overview**\n\nWe need the *shortest* palindrome that **starts with** the given string `s`.  \nIf we know the longest suffix of `s` that is already a palindrome,\nwe can build the required palindrome by:\n\n1. Take the part of `s` that precedes that suffix – call it `prefix`.\n2. Reverse `prefix` and append it to the end of `s`.\n\nWhy does this work?\n\n* Let `S = s + reverse(prefix)` be the candidate palindrome.\n* The first `|prefix|` characters of `S` are exactly `s`, so `S` begins with `s`.\n* The last `|prefix|` characters are the reverse of `prefix`, therefore the whole string reads the same forward and backward → it is a palindrome.\n\nTo obtain the *shortest* such palindrome we must choose the **longest** suffix of `s` that is already a palindrome.  \nScanning the string from left to right and stopping at the first position where the suffix `s[i:]` is a palindrome gives us precisely that longest suffix (the smallest `i` yields the longest suffix).\n\n---\n\n### Algorithm\n```\nmake_palindrome(s):\n    n ← length of s\n    for i from 0 to n‑1:\n        if s[i:] is a palindrome:\n            prefix ← s[0 … i‑1]          // characters before the palindromic suffix\n            return s + reverse(prefix)\n    return s                               // (never reached because a single character is always a palindrome)\n```\n\n*`is_palindrome(t)`* is a tiny helper that compares `t` with its reverse.\n\nThe algorithm runs in `O(n²)` time in the worst case (checking each suffix for being a palindrome), which is perfectly fine for typical input sizes.\n\n---\n\n### Reference Implementation\n\n```python\ndef is_palindrome(string: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Return True if `string` reads the same forwards and backwards.\"\"\"\n    return string == string[::-1]\n\n\ndef make_palindrome(string: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Find the shortest palindrome that begins with the supplied string.\n\n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    string : str\n        The prefix that must appear at the beginning of the resulting palindrome.\n\n    Returns\n    -------\n    str\n        The shortest palindrome that starts with ``string``.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(string)\n    # Scan from the left; the first position where the suffix is a palindrome\n    # gives the longest palindromic suffix.\n    for i in range(n):\n        if is_palindrome(string[i:]):          # suffix s[i:] is a palindrome\n            prefix = string[:i]               # characters before the suffix\n            return string + prefix[::-1]      # reverse & append\n    # By the problem statement a single‑character string is always a palindrome,\n    # so the loop always finds something.\n    return string\n```\n\n#### Quick sanity checks\n\n```python\nassert make_palindrome(\"\") == \"\"\nassert make_palindrome(\"cat\") == \"catac\"\nassert make_palindrome(\"cata\") == \"catac\"\nassert make_palindrome(\"abba\") == \"abba\"\nassert make_palindrome(\"abcd\") == \"abcdcba\"\nprint(\"All tests passed.\")\n```\n\nThe function follows exactly the reasoning described above and satisfies the examples given in the prompt.",
