          "dimension": "coding",
          "prompt": "\n\ndef skjkasdkd(lst):\n    \"\"\"You are given a list of integers.\n    You need to find the largest prime value and return the sum of its digits.\n\n    Examples:\n    For lst = [0,3,2,1,3,5,7,4,5,5,5,2,181,32,4,32,3,2,32,324,4,3] the output should be 10\n    For lst = [1,0,1,8,2,4597,2,1,3,40,1,2,1,2,4,2,5,1] the output should be 25\n    For lst = [1,3,1,32,5107,34,83278,109,163,23,2323,32,30,1,9,3] the output should be 13\n    For lst = [0,724,32,71,99,32,6,0,5,91,83,0,5,6] the output should be 11\n    For lst = [0,81,12,3,1,21] the output should be 3\n    For lst = [0,8,1,2,1,7] the output should be 7\n    \"\"\"\n",
          "final_answer": "**Solution Explanation**\n\nFor every integer `x` in the given list we have to decide whether it is a prime\nnumber.  \nAmong all the prime numbers we keep the biggest one – this is the *largest prime\nvalue*.  \nFinally we compute the sum of the decimal digits of that prime and return it.\n\nThe steps are therefore:\n\n1. **Prime test** – a simple trial‑division up to √n.  \n   * `n < 2` → not prime  \n   * `n == 2` → prime  \n   * even numbers > 2 → not prime  \n   * otherwise try every odd divisor `d` from `3` to `⌊√n⌋`.  \n     If any divides `n`, it is composite; otherwise it is prime.\n\n2. **Scan the list** – keep the greatest prime seen so far (`max_prime`).  \n   When a new prime larger than the current `max_prime` is found we replace it.\n\n3. **Digit sum** – after the scan, convert `max_prime` to a string,\n   add the integer values of its characters and return the result.\n\nThe algorithm runs in  \n\n```\nO(N · √M)   (N = length of the list, M = largest number in the list)\n```\n\nwhich is easily fast enough for the usual input sizes.\n\n---\n\n#### Correctness Proof  \n\nWe prove that the algorithm returns exactly the required value.\n\n*Lemma 1*  \n`is_prime(p)` returns `True` iff `p` is a prime number.\n\n*Proof.*  \n- If `p < 2` the function returns `False`. All integers `< 2` are not prime.  \n- If `p == 2` the function returns `True`. 2 is prime.  \n- If `p` is even and `p > 2` the function returns `False`. Any even number > 2 has\n  divisor 2, hence not prime.  \n- For odd `p ≥ 3` the function checks every odd integer `d` with `3 ≤ d ≤ √p`.\n  - If `d` divides `p`, then `p` has a non‑trivial divisor and is composite.\n  - If none does, no integer `d` with `3 ≤ d ≤ √p` divides `p`; consequently `p`\n    has no divisor other than 1 and itself, i.e. `p` is prime. ∎\n\n\n\n*Lemma 2*  \nAfter processing the whole list, `max_prime` equals the largest prime value among\nall elements of the list.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe algorithm examines each element `x` in order.\n\n- Whenever `x` is prime (by Lemma&nbsp;1) and `x` is larger than the current\n  `max_prime`, we assign `max_prime ← x`.  \n  Thus after handling the first `k` elements, `max_prime` is the maximum prime\n  among those `k` elements.\n\n- By induction on `k`, after the last element the invariant holds:\n  `max_prime` is the maximum prime among the first `k` elements.\n\nWhen the loop finishes, `k = N` (the whole list), therefore `max_prime` is the\nmaximum prime among **all** list entries. ∎\n\n\n\n*Lemma 3*  \nThe value returned by the algorithm equals the sum of the digits of the largest\nprime in the list.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma&nbsp;2 the variable `max_prime` holds exactly that largest prime.\nThe final line converts it to a string and adds the integer values of each character,\nwhich is precisely the sum of its decimal digits. ∎\n\n\n\n*Theorem*  \nFor any input list `lst` the function `skjkasdkd(lst)` returns the sum of the digits\nof the largest prime number contained in `lst`.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom Lemma&nbsp;1 the primality test is correct.  \nLemma&nbsp;2 shows that after scanning the list `max_prime` is the largest prime\npresent.  \nLemma&nbsp;3 proves that the subsequent computation yields the required digit sum.\nTherefore the returned value satisfies the specification. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n#### Reference Implementation (Python 3)\n\n```python\ndef skjkasdkd(lst):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a list of integers, return the sum of the digits of the largest prime\n    number found in the list.\n\n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    lst : list[int]\n        List containing integers (may contain non‑prime values).\n\n    Returns\n    -------\n    int\n        Sum of the decimal digits of the largest prime in ``lst``.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    # ---------- helper: primality test ----------\n    def is_prime(n: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Return True if n is a prime, False otherwise.\"\"\"\n        if n < 2:\n            return False\n        if n == 2:\n            return True\n        if n % 2 == 0:\n            return False\n        # check odd divisors up to sqrt(n)\n        limit = int(n ** 0.5) + 1\n        for d in range(3, limit, 2):\n            if n % d == 0:\n                return False\n        return True\n\n    # ---------- main logic ----------\n    max_prime = None                     # will hold the biggest prime found\n    for x in lst:\n        if is_prime(x):\n            if max_prime is None or x > max_prime:\n                max_prime = x\n\n    # According to the problem statement there is always at least one prime.\n    # If for some reason none exist we would return 0, but this never happens here.\n    if max_prime is None:\n        return 0\n\n    # sum of the digits of max_prime\n    digit_sum = sum(int(ch) for ch in str(max_prime))\n    return digit_sum\n\n\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Simple sanity checks (can be removed or turned into proper unit tests)\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    test_cases = [\n        ([0, 3, 2, 1, 3, 5, 7, 4, 5, 5, 5, 2, 181, 32, 4, 32, 3, 2, 32, 324, 4, 3],\n         10),\n        ([1, 0, 1, 8, 2, 4597, 2, 1, 3, 40, 1, 2, 1, 2, 4, 2, 5, 1],\n         25),\n        ([1, 3, 1, 32, 5107, 34, 83278, 109, 163, 23, 2323, 32, 30, 1, 9, 3],\n         13),\n        ([0, 724, 32, 71, 99, 32, 6, 0, 5, 91, 83, 0, 5, 6],\n         11),\n        ([0, 81, 12, 3, 1, 21],\n         3),\n        ([0, 8, 1, 2, 1, 7],\n         7),\n    ]\n\n    for idx, (lst, expected) in enumerate(test_cases, 1):\n        result = skjkasdkd(lst)\n        assert result == expected, f\"Test {idx} failed: got {result}, expected {expected}\"\n    print(\"All tests passed.\")\n```\n\nThe program follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above and conforms to\nthe required function signature `skjkasdkd(lst)`.",
