          "case_id": "humaneval-is-sorted-126",
          "dimension": "coding",
          "prompt": "\ndef is_sorted(lst):\n    '''\n    Given a list of numbers, return whether or not they are sorted\n    in ascending order. If list has more than 1 duplicate of the same\n    number, return False. Assume no negative numbers and only integers.\n\n    Examples\n    is_sorted([5]) ➞ True\n    is_sorted([1, 2, 3, 4, 5]) ➞ True\n    is_sorted([1, 3, 2, 4, 5]) ➞ False\n    is_sorted([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]) ➞ True\n    is_sorted([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]) ➞ True\n    is_sorted([1, 3, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7]) ➞ False\n    is_sorted([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4]) ➞ True\n    is_sorted([1, 2, 2, 2, 3, 4]) ➞ False\n    '''\n",
          "final_answer": "Here is a Python function that meets the requirements:\n```python\ndef is_sorted(lst):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a list of numbers, return whether or not they are sorted\n    in ascending order. If list has more than 1 duplicate of the same\n    number, return False. Assume no negative numbers and only integers.\n\n    Args:\n        lst (list): A list of integers\n\n    Returns:\n        bool: Whether the list is sorted in ascending order\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(lst) == 1:\n        return True  # A list with one element is always sorted\n\n    for i in range(len(lst) - 1):\n        if lst[i] > lst[i + 1]:\n            return False  # If we find a pair that's not in ascending order, return False\n\n    # Check for duplicate numbers\n    if len(lst) != len(set(lst)):\n        return False  # If there are duplicates, return False\n\n    return True  # If we've made it this far, the list is sorted\n```\nHere's an explanation of the code:\n\n1. We first check if the list has only one element. If so, it's always sorted, so we return `True`.\n2. We then iterate over the list, checking each pair of adjacent elements. If we find a pair that's not in ascending order, we return `False`.\n3. After checking all pairs, we check if the list has any duplicate numbers by comparing its length to the length of a set created from the list. If there are duplicates, we return `False`.\n4. If we've made it this far, the list is sorted, so we return `True`.\n\nYou can test this function with the examples you provided:\n```python\nprint(is_sorted([5]))  # True\nprint(is_sorted([1, 2, 3, 4, 5]))  # True\nprint(is_sorted([1, 3, 2, 4, 5]))  # False\nprint(is_sorted([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]))  # True\nprint(is_sorted([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]))  # True\nprint(is_sorted([1, 3, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7]))  # False\nprint(is_sorted([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4]))  # True\nprint(is_sorted([1, 2, 2, 2, 3, 4]))  # False\n```",
