          "case_id": "math-ceiling-fraction-002",
          "dimension": "math",
          "prompt": "Find $x$ such that $\\lceil x \\rceil + x = \\dfrac{23}{7}$. Express $x$ as a common fraction.",
          "final_answer": "Let $n = \\lceil x \\rceil$, where $n$ is an integer. By the definition of the ceiling function, we have:\n$$n - 1 < x \\le n$$\n(Note: if $x$ is an integer, then $x = n$, so the inequality becomes $x \\le x$, which holds; but we'll check whether $x$ can be an integer separately.)\n\nFrom the given equation $\\lceil x \\rceil + x = \\dfrac{23}{7}$, we can write:\n$$x = \\frac{23}{7} - n$$\n\nSubstituting this expression for $x$ into the inequality $n - 1 < x \\le n$:\n$$n - 1 < \\frac{23}{7} - n \\le n$$\n\nThis splits into two inequalities:\n\n1. $\\dfrac{23}{7} - n \\le n$\n   $$\\frac{23}{7} \\le 2n \\implies n \\ge \\frac{23}{14} \\approx 1.64$$\n   Since $n$ is an integer, $n \\ge 2$.\n\n2. $n - 1 < \\dfrac{23}{7} - n$\n   $$2n < \\frac{23}{7} + 1 = \\frac{30}{7} \\implies n < \\frac{15}{7} \\approx 2.14$$\n   Since $n$ is an integer, $n \\le 2$.\n\nCombining these results, we get $n = 2$.\n\nNow substitute $n = 2$ back into the expression for $x$:\n$$x = \\frac{23}{7} - 2 = \\frac{23 - 14}{7} = \\frac{9}{7}$$\n\nLet's verify: $\\lceil \\frac{9}{7} \\rceil + \\frac{9}{7}$. Since $1 < \\frac{9}{7} < 2$, $\\lceil \\frac{9}{7} \\rceil = 2$. Thus,\n$$2 + \\frac{9}{7} = \\frac{14 + 9}{7} = \\frac{23}{7}$$\nThe solution is correct.\n\nThus, $x = \\dfrac{9}{7}$.",
