          "final_answer": "For the expression to have a domain of all real numbers, the denominator must never equal zero for any real value of $x$. The numerator is a polynomial, so it is defined for all real numbers and does not affect the domain.\n\nThe denominator is $x^2 + bx + 8$. This is a quadratic with leading coefficient $1$ (positive), so its graph is a parabola opening upward. For the quadratic to never be zero, it must not intersect the $x$-axis, which means its discriminant must be negative:\n\n$$\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac < 0$$\n\nSubstituting $a=1$, $b=b$, and $c=8$:\n\n$$b^2 - 4(1)(8) < 0$$\n$$b^2 - 32 < 0$$\n$$b^2 < 32$$\n\nTaking the square root of both sides:\n\n$$-\\sqrt{32} < b < \\sqrt{32}$$\n\nSince $\\sqrt{32} = \\sqrt{16 \\cdot 2} = 4\\sqrt{2} \\approx 5.657$, we have:\n\n$$-5.657 < b < 5.657$$\n\nThe greatest integer value of $b$ satisfying this inequality is $5$.\n\nChecking: for $b=5$, the denominator is $x^2+5x+8$, with discriminant $25-32=-7<0$, so it has no real roots. For $b=6$, the denominator is $x^2+6x+8=(x+2)(x+4)$, which is zero at $x=-2$ and $x=-4$, so the domain would not be all real numbers.\n\nThe greatest integer value of $b$ is $\\boxed{5}$.",
