          "case_id": "math-ceiling-fraction-002",
          "dimension": "math",
          "prompt": "Find $x$ such that $\\lceil x \\rceil + x = \\dfrac{23}{7}$. Express $x$ as a common fraction.",
          "final_answer": "The equation is \\(\\lceil x \\rceil + x = \\frac{23}{7}\\). Let \\(n = \\lceil x \\rceil\\), an integer. Then \\(x = \\frac{23}{7} - n\\). Since \\(n\\) is integer, \\(\\lceil x \\rceil = \\lceil \\frac{23}{7} - n \\rceil = \\lceil \\frac{23}{7} \\rceil - n\\). However, by definition \\(\\lceil x \\rceil = n\\), so \\(n = \\lceil \\frac{23}{7} \\rceil - n\\), giving \\(2n = \\lceil \\frac{23}{7} \\rceil\\). Compute \\(\\frac{23}{7} = 3 + \\frac{2}{7}\\), so \\(\\lceil \\frac{23}{7} \\rceil = 4\\). Thus \\(2n = 4\\), so \\(n = 2\\). Then \\(x = \\frac{23}{7} - 2 = \\frac{23}{7} - \\frac{14}{7} = \\frac{9}{7}\\). Verify: \\(\\lceil \\frac{9}{7} \\rceil = 2\\), sum \\(2 + \\frac{9}{7} = \\frac{14}{7} + \\frac{9}{7} = \\frac{23}{7}\\).\n\nThus \\(x = \\frac{9}{7}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{9}{7}}\n\\]",
