          "case_id": "math-prime-roots-020",
          "dimension": "math",
          "prompt": "Suppose the roots of the polynomial $x^2 - mx + n$ are positive prime integers (not necessarily distinct). Given that $m < 20,$ how many possible values of $n$ are there?",
          "final_answer": "The polynomial \\(x^2 - mx + n\\) has positive prime roots \\(p\\) and \\(q\\), so \\(p + q = m\\) and \\(pq = n\\). Given \\(m < 20\\), we require \\(p + q < 20\\). Thus, we need to count distinct values of \\(n = pq\\) where \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) are primes with \\(p \\leq q\\) and \\(p + q < 20\\).\n\nEnumerating all pairs \\((p, q)\\) with \\(p \\leq q\\) and \\(p + q \\leq 19\\) yields 18 unordered pairs:\n- \\((2,2)\\), \\((2,3)\\), \\((2,5)\\), \\((2,7)\\), \\((2,11)\\), \\((2,13)\\), \\((2,17)\\),\n- \\((3,3)\\), \\((3,5)\\), \\((3,7)\\), \\((3,11)\\), \\((3,13)\\),\n- \\((5,5)\\), \\((5,7)\\), \\((5,11)\\), \\((5,13)\\),\n- \\((7,7)\\), \\((7,11)\\).\n\nFor each pair, \\(n = pq\\) is distinct: 4, 6, 10, 14, 22, 26, 34, 9, 15, 21, 33, 39, 25, 35, 55, 65, 49, 77. Thus, there are 18 possible values of \\(n\\).\n\n\\boxed{18}",
