          "final_answer": "The domain of the rational function $\\frac{9x^3+4x^2+11x+7}{x^2+bx+8}$ consists of all real numbers except where the denominator is zero. For the domain to be all real numbers, the denominator $x^2+bx+8$ must have no real roots. This requires the discriminant of the quadratic to be negative:\n\n\\[\nb^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 8 < 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad b^2 - 32 < 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad b^2 < 32.\n\\]\n\nThus, $|b| < \\sqrt{32} \\approx 5.6569$. The greatest integer $b$ satisfying this condition is $5$, since $5^2 = 25 < 32$ and $6^2 = 36 > 32$. Therefore, the greatest integer value of $b$ is $5$.\n\n\\boxed{5}",
