          "case_id": "humaneval-is-sorted-126",
          "dimension": "coding",
          "prompt": "\ndef is_sorted(lst):\n    '''\n    Given a list of numbers, return whether or not they are sorted\n    in ascending order. If list has more than 1 duplicate of the same\n    number, return False. Assume no negative numbers and only integers.\n\n    Examples\n    is_sorted([5]) ➞ True\n    is_sorted([1, 2, 3, 4, 5]) ➞ True\n    is_sorted([1, 3, 2, 4, 5]) ➞ False\n    is_sorted([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]) ➞ True\n    is_sorted([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]) ➞ True\n    is_sorted([1, 3, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7]) ➞ False\n    is_sorted([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4]) ➞ True\n    is_sorted([1, 2, 2, 2, 3, 4]) ➞ False\n    '''\n",
          "final_answer": "To determine if a list of numbers is sorted in ascending order and satisfies any additional constraints (like duplicates), we need to perform two checks:\n\n1. **Sorted Order Check**: Verify that each element is less than or equal to the next element. If at any point an element is greater than the next, the list is not sorted.\n2. **Duplicate Constraint**: Ensure no number appears more than twice. If any number appears more than 2 times, the list is invalid.\n\nThe function returns `True` only if both conditions are satisfied. If the list has more than one occurrence of any number, it also returns `False` (since \"more than 1 duplicate\" here means more than 2 occurrences).\n\n```python\ndef is_sorted(lst):\n    # Check for more than 2 occurrences of any number\n    freq = {}\n    for num in lst:\n        freq[num] = freq.get(num, 0) + 1\n    for count in freq.values():\n        if count > 2:\n            return False\n\n    # Check sorted order\n    for i in range(len(lst) - 1):\n        if lst[i] > lst[i + 1]:\n            return False\n    return True\n```",
