          "dimension": "coding",
          "prompt": "from typing import List, Any\n\n\ndef filter_integers(values: List[Any]) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\" Filter given list of any python values only for integers\n    >>> filter_integers(['a', 3.14, 5])\n    [5]\n    >>> filter_integers([1, 2, 3, 'abc', {}, []])\n    [1, 2, 3]\n    \"\"\"\n",
          "final_answer": "Looking at the function signature and the examples, I need to implement a function that filters a list of arbitrary Python values to only include integers. Here's the implementation:\n\n```python\nfrom typing import List, Any\n\n\ndef filter_integers(values: List[Any]) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\" Filter given list of any python values only for integers\n    >>> filter_integers(['a', 3.14, 5])\n    [5]\n    >>> filter_integers([1, 2, 3, 'abc', {}, []])\n    [1, 2, 3]\n    \"\"\"\n    return [value for value in values if type(value) == int]\n```\n\nThis implementation uses:\n- `type(value) == int` to check if each element is an integer (not a boolean, as booleans are technically subclasses of int in Python)\n- A list comprehension to filter the values efficiently\n- The return type being `List[int]` as specified\n\nThe function will:\n1. Return only elements that are exactly of type `int`\n2. Preserve the original order from the input list\n3. Not include booleans (since `type(True)` and `type(False)` both return `bool`, but these aren't integers in this context)",
