          "prompt": "For all real numbers $r$ and $s$, define the mathematical operation $\\#$ such that the following conditions apply: $r\\ \\#\\ 0 = r, r\\ \\#\\ s = s\\ \\#\\ r$, and $(r + 1)\\ \\#\\ s = (r\\ \\#\\ s) + s + 1$. What is the value of $11\\ \\#\\ 5$?",
          "final_answer": "The operation is defined by three conditions:  \n- \\( r \\# 0 = r \\)  \n- \\( r \\# s = s \\# r \\) (commutativity)  \n- \\( (r + 1) \\# s = (r \\# s) + s + 1 \\)  \n\nFrom these, we derive the general formula. Using commutativity and the first property, \\( f(0, s) = s \\# 0 = s \\). The third property gives a recurrence: for fixed \\( s \\), \\( f(r + 1, s) = f(r, s) + s + 1 \\), so \\( f(r, s) = (s + 1) \\cdot r + s \\) for all real \\( r \\). This satisfies all conditions and is consistent with commutativity.\n\nThus, \\( r \\# s = (s + 1) \\cdot r + s \\).  \n\nFor \\( 11 \\# 5 \\):  \n\\[\n11 \\# 5 = (5 + 1) \\cdot 11 + 5 = 6 \\cdot 11 + 5 = 66 + 5 = 71.\n\\]\n\n\\boxed{71}",
