          "case_id": "humaneval-paren-groups-001",
          "dimension": "coding",
          "prompt": "from typing import List\n\n\ndef separate_paren_groups(paren_string: str) -> List[str]:\n    \"\"\" Input to this function is a string containing multiple groups of nested parentheses. Your goal is to\n    separate those group into separate strings and return the list of those.\n    Separate groups are balanced (each open brace is properly closed) and not nested within each other\n    Ignore any spaces in the input string.\n    >>> separate_paren_groups('( ) (( )) (( )( ))')\n    ['()', '(())', '(()())']\n    \"\"\"",
          "final_answer": "Looking at the problem, I need to:\n\n1. Remove all spaces from the input string\n2. Find balanced groups of parentheses that are not nested within each other\n3. Return each group as a separate string\n\nHere's the implementation:\n\n```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef separate_paren_groups(paren_string: str) -> List[str]:\n    \"\"\" Input to this function is a string containing multiple groups of nested parentheses. Your goal is to\n    separate those group into separate strings and return the list of those.\n    Separate groups are balanced (each open brace is properly closed) and not nested within each other\n    Ignore any spaces in the input string.\n    >>> separate_paren_groups('( ) (( )) (( )( ))')\n    ['()', '(())', '(()())']\n    \"\"\"\n    # Step 1: Remove all spaces\n    cleaned = ''.join(c for c in paren_string if c != ' ')\n    \n    result = []\n    start = 0\n    \n    # Step 2: Find balanced groups using a counter\n    for i, c in enumerate(cleaned):\n        if c == '(':\n            # Start of a potential group\n            if i == 0 or cleaned[i-1] != '(':\n                # This group starts here (not nested)\n                j = i\n                count = 1\n                while j < len(cleaned) and count > 0:\n                    if cleaned[j] == '(':\n                        count += 1\n                    elif cleaned[j] == ')':\n                        count -= 1\n                    j += 1\n                \n                # Extract the group from i to j-1\n                group = cleaned[i:j]\n                result.append(group)\n                \n                # Move start to the character right after this group\n                i = j - 1  # For the next iteration to find the next group\n    \n    return result\n```\n\n**Explanation:**\n- First, we strip all spaces from the input string\n- Then, we scan through the string character by character, tracking a counter of open parentheses\n- When we find an opening parenthesis that's either at the start or preceded by a non-parenthesis character, we know it starts a new group\n- We count until we find the closing parenthesis that brings the counter back to zero\n- The extracted group is added to the result list\n- We move to the next group by continuing from where we left off",
