          "case_id": "humaneval-is-sorted-126",
          "dimension": "coding",
          "prompt": "\ndef is_sorted(lst):\n    '''\n    Given a list of numbers, return whether or not they are sorted\n    in ascending order. If list has more than 1 duplicate of the same\n    number, return False. Assume no negative numbers and only integers.\n\n    Examples\n    is_sorted([5]) ➞ True\n    is_sorted([1, 2, 3, 4, 5]) ➞ True\n    is_sorted([1, 3, 2, 4, 5]) ➞ False\n    is_sorted([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]) ➞ True\n    is_sorted([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]) ➞ True\n    is_sorted([1, 3, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7]) ➞ False\n    is_sorted([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4]) ➞ True\n    is_sorted([1, 2, 2, 2, 3, 4]) ➞ False\n    '''\n",
          "final_answer": "Looking at the problem, I need to check two things:\n\n1. Whether the list is sorted in ascending order (each element should be >= the previous one)\n2. Whether any number appears more than twice (no more than one duplicate)\n\nHere's my solution:\n\n```python\ndef is_sorted(lst):\n    # Check 1: Is the list sorted in ascending order?\n    for i in range(1, len(lst)):\n        if lst[i] < lst[i-1]:\n            return False\n    \n    # Check 2: No number appears more than twice\n    count = {}\n    for num in lst:\n        count[num] = count.get(num, 0) + 1\n        if count[num] > 2:\n            return False\n    \n    return True\n```\n\nThis function:\n- First iterates through the list checking if it's sorted in ascending order\n- Then checks the frequency of each number (up to 2 occurrences allowed)\n- Returns `False` immediately if either condition fails\n- Returns `True` only if both conditions pass\n\nThe time complexity is O(n) since we iterate through the list twice, and space complexity is O(n) for the count dictionary.",
