          "final_answer": "To solve the inequality \\( x^2 - 5x - 4 \\le 10 \\), first rewrite it by moving all terms to one side:\n\n\\[\nx^2 - 5x - 4 - 10 \\le 0 \\implies x^2 - 5x - 14 \\le 0\n\\]\n\nNext, find the roots of the quadratic equation \\( x^2 - 5x - 14 = 0 \\) using the quadratic formula \\( x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = -5 \\), and \\( c = -14 \\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{5 \\pm \\sqrt{25 + 56}}{2} = \\frac{5 \\pm 9}{2}\n\\]\n\nThis gives the roots:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{5 + 9}{2} = 7 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x = \\frac{5 - 9}{2} = -2\n\\]\n\nSince the coefficient of \\( x^2 \\) is positive, the parabola opens upwards. The quadratic expression \\( x^2 - 5x - 14 \\) is less than or equal to zero between the roots \\( x = -2 \\) and \\( x = 7 \\).\n\nTesting intervals confirms the solution is between \\( -2 \\) and \\( 7 \\), inclusive. Thus, the solution in interval notation is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{[-2, 7]}\n\\]",
