          "case_id": "math-piecewise-continuity-000",
          "dimension": "math",
          "prompt": "Let \\[f(x) = \\left\\{\n\\begin{array}{cl} ax+3, &\\text{ if }x>2, \\\\\nx-5 &\\text{ if } -2 \\le x \\le 2, \\\\\n2x-b &\\text{ if } x <-2.\n\\end{array}\n\\right.\\]Find $a+b$ if the piecewise function is continuous (which means that its graph can be drawn without lifting your pencil from the paper).",
          "final_answer": "To ensure the piecewise function \\( f(x) \\) is continuous, we need to check continuity at the transition points \\( x = -2 \\) and \\( x = 2 \\).\n\n**At \\( x = -2 \\):**\n- Left-hand limit (from \\( x < -2 \\)): \\( 2(-2) - b = -4 - b \\)\n- Right-hand limit (from \\( -2 \\leq x \\leq 2 \\)): \\( -2 - 5 = -7 \\)\n\nFor continuity:\n\\[\n-4 - b = -7 \\implies b = 3\n\\]\n\n**At \\( x = 2 \\):**\n- Left-hand limit (from \\( -2 \\leq x \\leq 2 \\)): \\( 2 - 5 = -3 \\)\n- Right-hand limit (from \\( x > 2 \\)): \\( a(2) + 3 = 2a + 3 \\)\n\nFor continuity:\n\\[\n2a + 3 = -3 \\implies 2a = -6 \\implies a = -3\n\\]\n\nThus, \\( a = -3 \\) and \\( b = 3 \\). Therefore, \\( a + b = -3 + 3 = 0 \\).\n\nAnswer: \\(\\boxed{0}\\)",
