          "prompt": "Find the greatest integer value of $b$ for which the expression $\\frac{9x^3+4x^2+11x+7}{x^2+bx+8}$ has a domain of all real numbers.",
          "final_answer": "To ensure the expression \\(\\frac{9x^3+4x^2+11x+7}{x^2+bx+8}\\) has a domain of all real numbers, the denominator \\(x^2 + bx + 8\\) must not have real roots. This requires the discriminant of the quadratic to be negative.  \n\nThe discriminant \\(D\\) of \\(x^2 + bx + 8\\) is \\(b^2 - 4ac = b^2 - 32\\). For no real roots, \\(D < 0\\), so \\(b^2 < 32\\). The greatest integer \\(b\\) satisfying this is the largest integer less than \\(\\sqrt{32} \\approx 5.656\\), which is \\(5\\).  \n\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
