          "case_id": "math-ceiling-fraction-002",
          "dimension": "math",
          "prompt": "Find $x$ such that $\\lceil x \\rceil + x = \\dfrac{23}{7}$. Express $x$ as a common fraction.",
          "final_answer": "To solve the equation \\(\\lceil x \\rceil + x = \\dfrac{23}{7}\\), we need to understand the properties of the ceiling function \\(\\lceil x \\rceil\\). The ceiling function \\(\\lceil x \\rceil\\) is the smallest integer greater than or equal to \\(x\\). Therefore, if \\(x\\) is a real number, then \\(\\lceil x \\rceil\\) is an integer and \\(x\\) must be in the interval \\([n, n+1)\\) for some integer \\(n\\).\n\nLet's denote \\(\\lceil x \\rceil\\) by \\(n\\). Then \\(n\\) is an integer and \\(x\\) is in the interval \\([n, n+1)\\). The equation becomes:\n\\[ n + x = \\frac{23}{7} \\]\nSince \\(x\\) is in the interval \\([n, n+1)\\), we have:\n\\[ n \\leq x < n+1 \\]\nSubstituting \\(x = \\frac{23}{7} - n\\) into the inequality, we get:\n\\[ n \\leq \\frac{23}{7} - n < n+1 \\]\nThis can be split into two inequalities:\n1. \\(n \\leq \\frac{23}{7} - n\\)\n2. \\(\\frac{23}{7} - n < n+1\\)\n\nLet's solve the first inequality:\n\\[ n \\leq \\frac{23}{7} - n \\]\n\\[ 2n \\leq \\frac{23}{7} \\]\n\\[ n \\leq \\frac{23}{14} \\]\nSince \\(n\\) is an integer, the possible values for \\(n\\) are 1 or 2.\n\nNow let's solve the second inequality:\n\\[ \\frac{23}{7} - n < n+1 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{23}{7} - 1 < 2n \\]\n\\[ \\frac{23}{7} - \\frac{7}{7} < 2n \\]\n\\[ \\frac{16}{7} < 2n \\]\n\\[ \\frac{8}{7} < n \\]\nSince \\(n\\) is an integer, the possible values for \\(n\\) are 2.\n\nSo, the only possible value for \\(n\\) is 2. Now we substitute \\(n = 2\\) back into the equation \\(x = \\frac{23}{7} - n\\):\n\\[ x = \\frac{23}{7} - 2 = \\frac{23}{7} - \\frac{14}{7} = \\frac{9}{7} \\]\nWe need to check if \\(x = \\frac{9}{7}\\) satisfies the original equation:\n\\[ \\lceil \\frac{9}{7} \\rceil + \\frac{9}{7} = 2 + \\frac{9}{7} = \\frac{14}{7} + \\frac{9}{7} = \\frac{23}{7} \\]\nSince the equation holds true, the solution is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{9}{7}} \\]",
