          "dimension": "math",
          "prompt": "For what values of $x$ is it true that $x^2 - 5x - 4 \\le 10$? Express your answer in interval notation.",
          "final_answer": "To solve the inequality \\(x^2 - 5x - 4 \\le 10\\), we first need to rewrite it in a standard quadratic form. We do this by moving all terms to one side of the inequality:\n\n\\[x^2 - 5x - 4 - 10 \\le 0\\]\n\\[x^2 - 5x - 14 \\le 0\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the roots of the quadratic equation \\(x^2 - 5x - 14 = 0\\). We can do this by factoring the quadratic expression. We look for two numbers that multiply to \\(-14\\) and add up to \\(-5\\). These numbers are \\(-7\\) and \\(2\\). Therefore, we can factor the quadratic as follows:\n\n\\[(x - 7)(x + 2) = 0\\]\n\nSetting each factor equal to zero gives us the roots:\n\n\\[x - 7 = 0 \\implies x = 7\\]\n\\[x + 2 = 0 \\implies x = -2\\]\n\nThe roots of the quadratic equation are \\(x = 7\\) and \\(x = -2\\). These roots divide the real number line into three intervals: \\((-\\infty, -2)\\), \\((-2, 7)\\), and \\((7, \\infty)\\). We need to test each interval to determine where the quadratic expression \\(x^2 - 5x - 14\\) is less than or equal to zero.\n\n1. For \\(x \\in (-\\infty, -2)\\), we can choose \\(x = -3\\):\n   \\[(-3)^2 - 5(-3) - 14 = 9 + 15 - 14 = 10 > 0\\]\n   So, \\(x^2 - 5x - 14 > 0\\) in this interval.\n\n2. for \\(x \\in (-2, 7)\\), we can choose \\(x = 0\\):\n   \\[0^2 - 5(0) - 14 = -14 < 0\\]\n   so, \\(x^2 - 5x - 14 \\le 0\\) in this interval.\n\n3. for \\(x \\in (7, \\infty)\\), we can choose \\(x = 8\\):\n   \\[8^2 - 5(8) - 14 = 64 - 40 - 14 = 10 > 0\\]\n   so, \\(x^2 - 5x - 14 > 0\\) in this interval.\n\nSince the quadratic expression is less than or equal to zero in the interval \\([-2, 7]\\), the solution to the inequality \\(x^2 - 5x - 4 \\le 10\\) is:\n\n\\[\\boxed{[-2, 7]}\\]",
