          "case_id": "math-prime-roots-020",
          "dimension": "math",
          "prompt": "Suppose the roots of the polynomial $x^2 - mx + n$ are positive prime integers (not necessarily distinct). Given that $m < 20,$ how many possible values of $n$ are there?",
          "final_answer": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the possible values of \\( n \\) for the polynomial \\( x^2 - mx + n \\) given that its roots are positive prime integers and \\( m < 20 \\).\n\nLet's denote the roots of the polynomial by \\( p \\) and \\( q \\), where \\( p \\) and \\( q \\) are positive prime integers. By Vieta's formulas, we know that:\n\\[\nm = p + q\n\\]\n\\[\nn = pq\n\\]\n\nGiven \\( m < 20 \\), we have:\n\\[\np + q < 20\n\\]\n\nWe need to find all pairs of prime numbers \\( (p, q) \\) such that \\( p + q < 20 \\). Let's list the prime numbers less than 20:\n\\[\n2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19\n\\]\n\nWe will consider all possible pairs of these primes and check if their sum is less than 20.\n\n1. \\( (2, 3) \\): \\( 2 + 3 = 5 \\) (valid)\n2. \\( (2, 5) \\): \\( 2 + 5 = 7 \\) (valid)\n3. \\( (2, 7) \\): \\( 2 + 7 = 9 \\) (valid)\n4. \\( (2, 11) \\): \\( 2 + 11 = 13 \\) (valid)\n5. \\( (2, 13) \\): \\( 2 + 13 = 15 \\) (valid)\n6. \\( (2, 17) \\): \\( 2 + 17 = 19 \\) (valid)\n7. \\( (2, 19) \\): \\( 2 + 19 = 21 \\) (not valid)\n8. \\( (3, 5) \\): \\( 3 + 5 = 8 \\) (valid)\n9. \\( (3, 7) \\): \\( 3 + 7 = 10 \\) (valid)\n10. \\( (3, 11) \\): \\( 3 + 11 = 14 \\) (valid)\n11. \\( (3, 13) \\): \\( 3 + 13 = 16 \\) (valid)\n12. \\( (3, 17) \\): \\( 3 + 17 = 20 \\) (not valid)\n13. \\( (3, 19) \\): \\( 3 + 19 = 22 \\) (not valid)\n14. \\( (5, 7) \\): \\( 5 + 7 = 12 \\) (valid)\n15. \\( (5, 11) \\): \\( 5 + 11 = 16 \\) (valid)\n16. \\( (5, 13) \\): \\( 5 + 13 = 18 \\) (valid)\n17. \\( (5, 17) \\): \\( 5 + 17 = 22 \\) (not valid)\n18. \\( (5, 19) \\): \\( 5 + 19 = 24 \\) (not valid)\n19. \\( (7, 11) \\): \\( 7 + 11 = 18 \\) (valid)\n20. \\( (7, 13) \\): \\( 7 + 13 = 20 \\) (not valid)\n21. \\( (7, 17) \\): \\( 7 + 17 = 24 \\) (not valid)\n22. \\( (7, 19) \\): \\( 7 + 19 = 26 \\) (not valid)\n23. \\( (11, 13) \\): \\( 11 + 13 = 24 \\) (not valid)\n24. \\( (11, 17) \\): \\( 11 + 17 = 28 \\) (not valid)\n25. \\( (11, 19) \\): \\( 11 + 19 = 30 \\) (not valid)\n26. \\( (13, 17) \\): \\( 13 + 17 = 30 \\) (not valid)\n27. \\( (13, 19) \\): \\( 13 + 19 = 32 \\) (not valid)\n28. \\( (17, 19) \\): \\( 17 + 19 = 36 \\) (not valid)\n\nFrom the above, the valid pairs are:\n\\[\n(2, 3), (2, 5), (2, 7), (2, 11), (2, 13), (2, 17), (3, 5), (3, 7), (3, 11), (3, 13), (5, 7), (5, 11), (5, 13), (7, 11)\n\\]\n\nNow, we calculate \\( n = pq \\) for each valid pair:\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n(2, 3) & : n = 2 \\times 3 = 6 \\\\\n(2, 5) & : n = 2 \\times 5 = 10 \\\\\n(2, 7) & : n = 2 \\times 7 = 14 \\\\\n(2, 11) & : n = 2 \\times 11 = 22 \\\\\n(2, 13) & : n = 2 \\times 13 = 26 \\\\\n(2, 17) & : n = 2 \\times 17 = 34 \\\\\n(3, 5) & : n = 3 \\times 5 = 15 \\\\\n(3, 7) & : n = 3 \\times 7 = 21 \\\\\n(3, 11) & : n = 3 \\times 11 = 33 \\\\\n(3, 13) & : n = 3 \\times 13 = 39 \\\\\n(5, 7) & : n = 5 \\times 7 = 35 \\\\\n(5, 11) & : n = 5 \\times 11 = 55 \\\\\n(5, 13) & : n = 5 \\times 13 = 65 \\\\\n(7, 11) & : n = 7 \\times 11 = 77 \\\\\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nThe distinct values of \\( n \\) are:\n\\[\n6, 10, 14, 15, 21, 22, 26, 33, 34, 35, 39, 55, 65, 77\n\\]\n\nCounting these, we find there are 14 distinct values of \\( n \\).\n\nThus, the number of possible values of \\( n \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{14}\n\\]",
