          "case_id": "math-piecewise-continuity-000",
          "dimension": "math",
          "prompt": "Let \\[f(x) = \\left\\{\n\\begin{array}{cl} ax+3, &\\text{ if }x>2, \\\\\nx-5 &\\text{ if } -2 \\le x \\le 2, \\\\\n2x-b &\\text{ if } x <-2.\n\\end{array}\n\\right.\\]Find $a+b$ if the piecewise function is continuous (which means that its graph can be drawn without lifting your pencil from the paper).",
          "final_answer": "To determine the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) such that the piecewise function \\(f(x)\\) is continuous, we need to ensure that the function values match at the points where the definitions of the function change, specifically at \\(x = 2\\) and \\(x = -2\\).\n\nThe function is defined as:\n\\[ f(x) = \\left\\{\n\\begin{array}{cl} ax+3, &\\text{ if }x>2, \\\\\nx-5 &\\text{ if } -2 \\le x \\le 2, \\\\\n2x-b &\\text{ if } x <-2.\n\\end{array}\n\\right. \\]\n\n### Continuity at \\(x = 2\\):\nFor the function to be continuous at \\(x = 2\\), the left-hand limit as \\(x\\) approaches 2 from the left must equal the right-hand limit as \\(x\\) approaches 2 from the right, and both must equal the value of the function at \\(x = 2\\).\n\nThe left-hand limit as \\(x\\) approaches 2 from the left is:\n\\[ \\lim_{x \\to 2^-} f(x) = 2 - 5 = -3. \\]\n\nThe right-hand limit as \\(x\\) approaches 2 from the right is:\n\\[ \\lim_{x \\to 2^+} f(x) = a(2) + 3 = 2a + 3. \\]\n\nFor the function to be continuous at \\(x = 2\\), we need:\n\\[ 2a + 3 = -3. \\]\nSolving for \\(a\\):\n\\[ 2a + 3 = -3 \\implies 2a = -6 \\implies a = -3. \\]\n\n### continuity at \\(x = -2\\):\nFor the function to be continuous at \\(x = -2\\), the left-hand limit as \\(x\\) approaches -2 from the left must equal the right-hand limit as \\(x\\) approaches -2 from the right, and both must equal the value of the function at \\(x = -2\\).\n\nThe left-hand limit as \\(x\\) approaches -2 from the left is:\n\\[ \\lim_{x \\to -2^-} f(x) = -2 - 5 = -7. \\]\n\nThe right-hand limit as \\(x\\) approaches -2 from the right is:\n\\[ \\lim_{x \\to -2^+} f(x) = 2(-2) - b = -4 - b. \\]\n\nFor the function to be continuous at \\(x = -2\\), we need:\n\\[ -4 - b = -7. \\]\nsolving for \\(b\\):\n\\[ -4 - b = -7 \\implies -b = -3 \\implies b = 3. \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\nThe values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) that make the function continuous are \\(a = -3\\) and \\(b = 3\\). Therefore, the sum \\(a + b\\) is:\n\\[ a + b = -3 + 3 = 0. \\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{0}. \\]",
          "passed": true,
