          "dimension": "math",
          "prompt": "Find the greatest integer value of $b$ for which the expression $\\frac{9x^3+4x^2+11x+7}{x^2+bx+8}$ has a domain of all real numbers.",
          "final_answer": "To determine the greatest integer value of \\( b \\) for which the expression \\(\\frac{9x^3 + 4x^2 + 11x + 7}{x^2 + bx + 8}\\) has a domain of all real numbers, we need to ensure that the denominator \\( x^2 + bx + 8 \\) does not have any real roots. This means the quadratic equation \\( x^2 + bx + 8 = 0 \\) should have a negative discriminant.\n\nThe discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of a quadratic equation \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\n\\]\nFor the quadratic equation \\( x^2 + bx + 8 = 0 \\), we have \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = b \\), and \\( c = 8 \\). Therefore, the discriminant is:\n\\[\n\\Delta = b^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 8 = b^2 - 32\n\\]\nFor the quadratic equation to have no real roots, the discriminant must be less than zero:\n\\[\nb^2 - 32 < 0\n\\]\nSolving this inequality:\n\\[\nb^2 < 32\n\\]\nTaking the square root of both sides, we get:\n\\[\n|b| < \\sqrt{32}\n\\]\nSince \\(\\sqrt{32} = 4\\sqrt{2} \\approx 5.656\\), we have:\n\\[\n-5.656 < b < 5.656\n\\]\nThe greatest integer value of \\( b \\) within this range is \\( 5 \\).\n\nThus, the greatest integer value of \\( b \\) for which the expression \\(\\frac{9x^3 + 4x^2 + 11x + 7}{x^2 + bx + 8}\\) has a domain of all real numbers is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{5}\n\\]",
