          "case_id": "math-piecewise-continuity-000",
          "dimension": "math",
          "prompt": "Let \\[f(x) = \\left\\{\n\\begin{array}{cl} ax+3, &\\text{ if }x>2, \\\\\nx-5 &\\text{ if } -2 \\le x \\le 2, \\\\\n2x-b &\\text{ if } x <-2.\n\\end{array}\n\\right.\\]Find $a+b$ if the piecewise function is continuous (which means that its graph can be drawn without lifting your pencil from the paper).",
          "final_answer": "For a piecewise function to be continuous, the limits of the function as $x$ approaches the boundaries of the intervals must exist and be equal to the value of the function at those points. In other words, the \"pieces\" of the graph must meet perfectly without any gaps or jumps.\n\nThe function is defined in three parts with boundary points at $x = 2$ and $x = -2$. We need to ensure continuity at these two points.\n\n**Step 1: Continuity at $x = 2$**\nAt $x = 2$, the function switches from the middle piece ($f(x) = x - 5$ for $-2 \\le x \\le 2$) to the top piece ($f(x) = ax + 3$ for $x > 2$).\n\n1.  **Left-hand limit** (approaching 2 from the left, using the middle piece):\n    $$ \\lim_{x \\to 2^-} f(x) = 2 - 5 = -3 $$\n2.  **Right-hand limit** (approaching 2 from the right, using the top piece):\n    $$ \\lim_{x \\to 2^+} f(x) = a(2) + 3 = 2a + 3 $$\n\nFor continuity, these limits must be equal:\n$$ -3 = 2a + 3 $$\nSubtract 3 from both sides:\n$$ -6 = 2a $$\nDivide by 2:\n$$ a = -3 $$\n\n**Step 2: Continuity at $x = -2$**\nAt $x = -2$, the function switches from the bottom piece ($f(x) = 2x - b$ for $x < -2$) to the middle piece ($f(x) = x - 5$ for $-2 \\le x \\le 2$).\n\n1.  **Right-hand limit** (approaching -2 from the right, using the middle piece):\n    $$ \\lim_{x \\to -2^+} f(x) = -2 - 5 = -7 $$\n2.  **Left-hand limit** (approaching -2 from the left, using the bottom piece):\n    $$ \\lim_{x \\to -2^-} f(x) = 2(-2) - b = -4 - b $$\n\nFor continuity, these limits must be equal:\n$$ -7 = -4 - b $$\nAdd 4 to both sides:\n$$ -3 = -b $$\nMultiply by -1:\n$$ b = 3 $$\n\n**Step 3: Calculate $a + b$**\nNow that we have the values for $a$ and $b$:\n$$ a = -3 $$\n$$ b = 3 $$\n\n$$ a + b = -3 + 3 = 0 $$\n\nThe value of $a+b$ is 0.",
