          "dimension": "coding",
          "prompt": "\ndef is_sorted(lst):\n    '''\n    Given a list of numbers, return whether or not they are sorted\n    in ascending order. If list has more than 1 duplicate of the same\n    number, return False. Assume no negative numbers and only integers.\n\n    Examples\n    is_sorted([5]) ➞ True\n    is_sorted([1, 2, 3, 4, 5]) ➞ True\n    is_sorted([1, 3, 2, 4, 5]) ➞ False\n    is_sorted([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]) ➞ True\n    is_sorted([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]) ➞ True\n    is_sorted([1, 3, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7]) ➞ False\n    is_sorted([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4]) ➞ True\n    is_sorted([1, 2, 2, 2, 3, 4]) ➞ False\n    '''\n",
          "final_answer": "```python\ndef is_sorted(lst):\n    '''\n    Given a list of numbers, return whether or not they are sorted\n    in ascending order. If list has more than 1 duplicate of the same\n    number, return False. Assume no negative numbers and only integers.\n\n    Examples\n    is_sorted([5]) ➞ True\n    is_sorted([1, 2, 3, 4, 5]) ➞ True\n    is_sorted([1, 3, 2, 4, 5]) ➞ False\n    is_sorted([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]) ➞ True\n    is_sorted([1, 3, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7]) ➞ False\n    is_sorted([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4]) ➞ True\n    is_sorted([1, 2, 2, 2, 3, 4]) ➞ False\n    '''\n    # Check if list is sorted in ascending order and no number appears more than twice\n    for i in range(len(lst) - 1):\n        if lst[i] > lst[i + 1]:\n            return False\n        if lst[i] == lst[i + 1]:\n            count = 0\n            for j in range(i, len(lst)):\n                if lst[j] == lst[i]:\n                    count += 1\n                else:\n                    break\n            if count > 2:\n                return False\n    return True\n```\n\nThis function checks two conditions:\n1. The list must be sorted in ascending order (each element ≤ the next).\n2. No number may appear more than twice. If any number appears three or more times, it returns `False`.\n\n### Alternative Efficient Implementation\n\nFor better performance and clarity, here's an optimized version using a frequency map:\n\n```python\ndef is_sorted(lst):\n    '''\n    Given a list of numbers, return whether or not they are sorted\n    in ascending order. If list has more than 1 duplicate of the same\n    number, return False. Assume no negative numbers and only integers.\n    '''\n    if len(lst) <= 2:\n        return True\n\n    # Check for duplicates using a frequency dictionary\n    freq = {}\n    for num in lst:\n        freq[num] = freq.get(num, 0) + 1\n        if freq[num] > 2:\n            return False\n\n    # Check if sorted\n    for i in range(len(lst) - 1):\n        if lst[i] > lst[i + 1]:\n            return False\n\n    return True\n```\n\n### Test Cases\n\n```python\nprint(is_sorted([5]))                          # True\nprint(is_sorted([1, 2, 3, 4, 5]))             # True\nprint(is_sorted([1, 3, 2, 4, 5]))             # False\nprint(is_sorted([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]))           # True\nprint(is_sorted([1, 3, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7]))       # False\nprint(is_sorted([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4]))           # True\nprint(is_sorted([1, 2, 2, 2, 3, 4]))           # False\n```\n\nBoth implementations satisfy the requirements: they verify ascending order and ensure no number appears more than twice.",
