          "case_id": "arc-carbon-reservoir-070",
          "dimension": "reasoning",
          "prompt": "In the carbon cycle, carbon moves from reservoir to reservoir as various organic and inorganic processes occur on Earth. Only a small percentage of the global carbon is moved in this cycle each year. The remaining carbon is stored in these reservoirs. Which reservoir contains the greatest amount of stored carbon?\n\nChoices:\nA. plant biomass\nB. the atmosphere\nC. fossil fuels\nD. the deep ocean\n\nThink step by step in 2-4 sentences, then give Final answer: <letter>.",
          "final_answer": "The carbon cycle involves carbon moving between various reservoirs. The reservoir that stores the greatest amount of carbon over geological time and is distinct from active biological processes (like plant biomass) or atmospheric exchange is often cited in educational contexts. Among the choices, fossil fuels (coal, oil, and gas) represent a significant mass of stored carbon that was sequestered over millions of years. While the ocean also stores significant amounts, in many standard educational frameworks distinguishing between active and geological storage, fossil fuels are frequently identified as the reservoir with the greatest amount of permanently stored carbon compared to living plants and atmospheric gases.\n\nFinal answer: C",
