          "case_id": "math-prime-roots-020",
          "dimension": "math",
          "prompt": "Suppose the roots of the polynomial $x^2 - mx + n$ are positive prime integers (not necessarily distinct). Given that $m < 20,$ how many possible values of $n$ are there?",
          "final_answer": "The roots of the polynomial $x^2 - mx + n = 0$ are $p_1$ and $p_2$. Since the roots are positive prime integers, we can write $p_1$ and $p_2$ as primes from the set $P = \\{2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19, \\dots\\}$.\nFrom Vieta's formulas, we have:\n$m = p_1 + p_2$\n$n = p_1 p_2$\nWe are given the condition $m < 20$.\n\nWe need to find all possible values of $n$ derived from pairs of prime numbers $(p_1, p_2)$ such that their sum is less than 20. We iterate over all possible sums $m$ and check for valid pairs.\n\nThe possible integer values for $m$ range from the minimum sum of two primes to just below 20. Since the smallest prime is 2, the minimum sum is $2+2=4$. The maximum sum is 19 (since $m < 20$).\nWe list all pairs $(p_1, p_2)$ such that $p_1 + p_2 = m$:\n\n1.  **$m = 4$**: $(2, 2) \\implies n = 2 \\times 2 = 4$\n2.  **$m = 5$**: $(2, 3) \\implies n = 2 \\times 3 = 6$\n3.  **$m = 6$**: $(3, 3) \\implies n = 3 \\times 3 = 9$\n4.  **$m = 7$**: $(2, 5) \\implies n = 2 \\times 5 = 10$\n5.  **$m = 8$**: $(3, 5) \\implies n = 3 \\times 5 = 15$\n6.  **$m = 9$**: $(2, 7) \\implies n = 2 \\times 7 = 14$\n7.  **$m = 10$**: $(3, 7) \\implies n = 3 \\times 7 = 21$\n8.  **$m = 11$**: No prime pair sums to 11.\n9.  **$m = 12$**: $(5, 7) \\implies n = 5 \\times 7 = 35$\n10. **$m = 13$**: $(2, 11) \\implies n = 2 \\times 11 = 22$\n11. **$m = 14$**: $(3, 11) \\implies n = 3 \\times 11 = 33$\n12. **$m = 15$**: $(2, 13) \\implies n = 2 \\times 13 = 26$\n13. **$m = 16$**: $(3, 13) \\implies n = 3 \\times 13 = 39$ and $(5, 11) \\implies n = 5 \\times 11 = 55$\n14. **$m = 17$**: No prime pair sums to 17.\n15. **$m = 18$**: $(5, 13) \\implies n = 5 \\times 13 = 65$ and $(7, 11) \\implies n = 7 \\times 11 = 77$\n16. **$m = 19$**: $(2, 17) \\implies n = 2 \\times 17 = 34$\n\nWe collect all the distinct values of $n$:\n$\\{4, 6, 9, 10, 14, 15, 21, 22, 26, 33, 34, 35, 39, 55, 65, 77\\}$.\n\nCounting these distinct values, we get a total of 15.\n\nThe number of possible values of $n$ is 15.\n\n15",
