          "case_id": "math-exponential-offer-021",
          "dimension": "math",
          "prompt": "Mike paid $\\$1.25$ for a stamp three years ago. He was just offered double that amount for the stamp. Assuming the stamp's offer price doubles every three years, how many dollars will he be offered in 12 more years?",
          "final_answer": "Here is the step-by-step calculation to find the answer:\n\n1.  **Determine the current offer price:**\n    Mike was just offered double the amount he paid three years ago.\n    $$ \\text{Current Offer} = \\$1.25 \\times 2 = \\$2.50 $$\n\n2.  **Calculate the number of growth periods:**\n    The problem states that the offer price doubles every three years. We need to find out how many \"three-year\" intervals are in the time frame of 12 years.\n    $$ \\text{Periods} = \\frac{12 \\text{ years}}{3 \\text{ years/period}} = 4 \\text{ periods} $$\n\n3.  **Calculate the final offer price:**\n    We start with the current offer price ($\\$2.50$) and apply the doubling rule for each of the 4 periods.\n    $$ \\text{Final Price} = \\$2.50 \\times 2^4 $$\n    $$ \\text{Final Price} = \\$2.50 \\times 16 $$\n    $$ \\text{Final Price} = \\$40.00 $$\n\n**Answer:**\nMike will be offered **$40.00** in 12 more years.",
