          "case_id": "math-piecewise-continuity-000",
          "dimension": "math",
          "prompt": "Let \\[f(x) = \\left\\{\n\\begin{array}{cl} ax+3, &\\text{ if }x>2, \\\\\nx-5 &\\text{ if } -2 \\le x \\le 2, \\\\\n2x-b &\\text{ if } x <-2.\n\\end{array}\n\\right.\\]Find $a+b$ if the piecewise function is continuous (which means that its graph can be drawn without lifting your pencil from the paper).",
          "final_answer": "To make the piecewise function continuous, the value of the function at every point must equal the limit of the function as it approaches that point. The points where the definition of the function changes are $x = -2$ and $x = 2$. We need to ensure continuity at these points.\n\n**1. Continuity at $x = -2$:**\nThe function is defined as $f(x) = x - 5$ for $-2 \\le x \\le 2$. Therefore, the value of the function at $x = -2$ is:\n$$f(-2) = -2 - 5 = -7$$\n\nFor the function to be continuous at $x = -2$, the limit as $x$ approaches $-2$ from the left must equal the value of the function at $x = -2$. The part of the function for $x < -2$ is given by $f(x) = 2x - b$.\n$$ \\lim_{x \\to -2^-} f(x) = 2(-2) - b = -4 - b $$\n\nSo, we set the limits equal:\n$$ -4 - b = -7 $$\n$$ -b = -3 $$\n$$ b = 3 $$\n\n**2. Continuity at $x = 2$:**\nThe function is defined as $f(x) = ax + 3$ for $x > 2$. Therefore, the limit as $x$ approaches $2$ from the right is:\n$$ \\lim_{x \\to 2^+} f(x) = a(2) + 3 = 2a + 3 $$\n\nFor the function to be continuous at $x = 2$, the limit as $x$ approaches $2$ from the left must equal the value of the function at $x = 2$. The part of the function for $-2 \\le x \\le 2$ is given by $f(x) = x - 5$.\n$$ f(2) = 2 - 5 = -3 $$\n\nSo, we set the limits equal:\n$$ 2a + 3 = -3 $$\n$$ 2a = -6 $$\n$$ a = -3 $$\n\n**3. Calculate $a+b$:**\nWe have found that $a = -3$ and $b = 3$.\n$$ a + b = -3 + 3 = 0 $$\n\nThus, the value of $a+b$ is 0.",
