          "case_id": "math-domain-greatest-b-042",
          "dimension": "math",
          "prompt": "Find the greatest integer value of $b$ for which the expression $\\frac{9x^3+4x^2+11x+7}{x^2+bx+8}$ has a domain of all real numbers.",
          "final_answer": "The expression is given by:\n$$ f(x) = \\frac{9x^3+4x^2+11x+7}{x^2+bx+8} $$\nFor this expression to have a domain of all real numbers, the denominator must not be zero for any real value of $x$. That is, the quadratic equation $x^2 + bx + 8 = 0$ must have no real solutions.\n\nThis condition is equivalent to saying that the discriminant $\\Delta$ of the quadratic equation must be strictly negative:\n$$ \\Delta = b^2 - 4(1)(8) < 0 $$\n$$ b^2 - 32 < 0 $$\n$$ b^2 < 32 $$\n\nTaking the square root of both sides (since we are considering real numbers), we get:\n$$ -\\sqrt{32} < b < \\sqrt{32} $$\n\nWe simplify $\\sqrt{32}$:\n$$ \\sqrt{32} = \\sqrt{16 \\times 2} = 4\\sqrt{2} $$\nSince $\\sqrt{2} \\approx 1.414$, we have:\n$$ \\sqrt{32} \\approx 4 \\times 1.414 = 5.656 $$\n\nSo the inequality is approximately:\n$$ -5.656 < b < 5.656 $$\n\nWe need to find the greatest integer value of $b$ that satisfies this inequality. The integers in the range $(-5.656, 5.656)$ are $\\{-5, -4, -3, \\dots, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5\\}$.\n\nThe greatest integer in this set is 5.\n\nThus, the greatest integer value of $b$ is 5.\n\n5",
